president biden's domestic agenda is  getting a big boost from congress last  night the house voted to advance several  of the president's top priorities  including a more than three trillion  dollar budget framework and a voting  rights bill lawmakers also set a date  for a vote on rebuilding the nation's  infrastructure nicole killian is on  capitol hill to unpack it all nicole  good morning hey good morning to you  anthony and that deal almost fell apart  after some resistance from moderate  democrats but for now the president's  build back better agenda appears to be  on track the house approved a three and  a half trillion dollar budget outline  that will be used to craft a broad  package with many of the president's key  initiatives from expanded child care to  education climate change and other  social programs it also sets up a vote  on a trillion dollar bipartisan  infrastructure bill by september 27th  that was a concession to moderates who  wanted a vote now lawmakers also passed  the john lewis voting rights advancement  act named after the late georgia  congressman the bill would restore  provisions in the 1965 voting rights act  that were struck down by the supreme  court it faces an uphill battle in the  senate where many republican leaders  have expressed opposition as for that  massive budget package democrats hope to  pass it next month alone but some  senators are balking at the price tag  and have threatened not to support it  tony we'll keep tracking it nicole thank  you very much
 

